  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Michigan received $5.3B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense obligated $2.6B and the General Services Administration obligated $1.9B in the same period. Obligations fell 20.1% year over year. Federal buying in Michigan included 148,677 awards with an average award size of $35.5K. Automobile Manufacturing accounted for $1.8B of obligations and Military Armored Vehicle, Tank, and Tank Component Manufacturing accounted for $1.3B. GENERAL DYNAMICS LAND SYSTEMS INC. received $1.3B, making it the largest vendor by obligations, followed by FORD MOTOR CO with $658M and GENERAL MOTORS LLC with $619M. Historical obligations show FY21 at $6.8B, FY22 at $9B, FY23 at $8.4B, FY24 at $8B, and FY25 at $6.6B. The figures are partial for the fiscal year to date and represent contract obligations through FY2026 to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies drive the most federal spending in Michigan?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer in Michigan, obligating $2.6B in FY2026 to date. The General Services Administration is the second largest buyer with $1.9B in obligations. Other agencies with notable obligations include the Department of Homeland Security with $202M and the Department of Veterans Affairs with $200M. Several civilian agencies each obligated between $99.4M and $28.2M in the period."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest contractors and which industries dominate?",
        "body": "GENERAL DYNAMICS LAND SYSTEMS INC. is the top vendor with $1.3B in obligations, followed by FORD MOTOR CO at $658M and GENERAL MOTORS LLC at $619M. Automobile Manufacturing leads industries with $1.8B, and Military Armored Vehicle, Tank, and Tank Component Manufacturing follows with $1.3B. Other industries with material obligations include Other Engine Equipment Manufacturing at $148M and Petroleum Refineries at $139M. The top vendors and industries reflect a strong vehicle and defense manufacturing concentration."
      }
    ]
  },
